334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (The crucial technical observation is that MCP, A2A, and ACP all use JSON-RPC 2.0 as their wire format. This means their message semantics are transport-agnostic by design. The JSON-RPC specification itself states that the protocol is transport-independent; the message format is the same whether carried over HTTP, WebSocket, stdio, or any other channel. This opens the door to running all three protocol semantics over atRPC without modifying the protocols themselves.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (5.1 MCP over atRPC)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
336 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (An MCP server that provides tools \(say, a database query tool\) can be wrapped so that its JSON-RPC 2.0 requests and responses are transported via atRPC instead of HTTP. The Pembrook project demonstrates this pattern with its mcp_servers/ directory, which contains wrappers for home automation, database, and web browser tools. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
